# Windows batch files must use CRLF line endings. cmd.exe's label scanner
# (goto/call :label) fails to resolve labels in an LF-only .cmd/.bat, so pin
# these to CRLF on every checkout regardless of the user's core.autocrlf.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# CPE-1042: the sample-data baseline must be byte-exact and reproducible on every
# platform. Treat all of samples/ as binary so git never applies EOL conversion
# (a CRLF-munged PDF/MP3/etc. would no longer match the generated baseline).
samples/** -text

# CPE-1880: the harness scripts under scripts/ are imported by vitest through Vite's
# transform pipeline, and a .mjs checked out with CRLF makes that transform throw
# `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` with a location that points at a comment
# on line 2 — i.e. no usable diagnostic at all. It reproduces ONLY on a checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true (Windows dev boxes); the Linux CI runner checks out LF and stays
# green, so CI cannot catch it and the next person loses an hour. Pin them to LF on
# every checkout regardless of the user's autocrlf setting, the same way the .cmd/.bat
# rule above pins the opposite direction for cmd.exe.
# CPE-1880 follow-up: `scripts/*.mjs` does not match subdirectories, so
# `scripts/dev-harness/**/check.mjs` was left unpinned.
#
# CPE-1906 round 2 stopped patching the directory list and pinned the FILE TYPE, because the list was
# wrong twice more. `src/lib/fixtures/ghStub.mjs` (a .mjs under src/) and
# `sidecar/agent-board/clickthrough.mjs` both matched no rule and both checked out CRLF against an LF
# blob — measured 131 and 201 CRLF respectively. Each was harmless only by accident (neither is
# imported through Vite's transform today), and "harmless until someone imports it" is not a property
# anyone maintains. This is CPE-1932's rule applied to a .gitattributes: name the class, not the
# instances you remembered. `src/lib/sprintStallControls.test.ts` enumerates every tracked *.mjs via
# `git ls-files` and asserts BOTH that this checkout is LF and that `git check-attr` reports `eol: lf`
# for each — so a new .mjs anywhere in the tree is covered on the day it lands.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# CPE-1893: the same CRLF-on-a-Windows-checkout risk CPE-1880 fixed for scripts/*.mjs applies here
# too, with a harsher failure mode — a CRLF .sh sourced/run by a GitHub Actions step on the
# Linux/macOS runners those workflows target fails at the shell level (a stray `\r` on the shebang
# or inside a function body), not merely at a JS transform. Pin every workflow helper script to LF
# on every checkout regardless of the user's core.autocrlf, covering both the pre-existing
# ffmpeg-anchor-check.sh (CPE-1796) and catalog-freshness-check.sh (CPE-1893) added alongside it.
.github/workflows/scripts/*.sh text eol=lf
